        "text": "On the basis of origin, ecological succession may be of following two types lichens on a rock\n\n• Primary succession - It is the establishment of plants on land that has not been previously vegetated. It begins with colonization and establishment of pioneer species.\n\n• Secondary succession - It is the invasion of a habitat by plants on land that was previously vegetated. Removal of past vegetation may be caused by natural or human disturbances such as fire, logging, cultivation, or hurricanes.\n\nWhereas on the basis of location, ecological succession may be xerarch or hydrarch: (tulip poplar)\n\nMature forest (while oak and hickorv Climax forest (beech and sugar maple)\n\nXerarch Succession: Ecological succession Plant succession starting on bare ground or rock and culminating in a mature climax forest is called xerarch succession. The pioneer specics, such as lichens and mosses, result in the gradual accumulation of soil.\n\nThe lichen colonies on these boulders are the first step in xerarch succession. Along with mosses, they trap dust particles and form a layer of soil on the rock surface that allows other plants, such as grasses and herbs, to become established. Hydrarch: Plant succession starting on relatively shallow water, such as ponds and lakes, and culminating in a mature forest is termed as hydrarch.",
