BIOLOGY_12_DATA[2]["englishSummary"]="The skeleton in animals contributes in upholding and sustaining the body against gravity and other external forces.\n\nThe vertebrate skeleton is composed either of cartilage or bone.\n\nCartilage consists of cells called chondrocytes and a tough, flexible matrix made of type II collagen and it is without blood vessels.\n\nBone is a living hard and strong structure consisting of a hard ground substance or matrix and cells.\n\nBones are composed of osteoblasts (cells that help form bone), and osteoclasts (cells that help eat away old bone) and osteocytes which are mature osteoblasts.\n\nHuman skeleton consists of 206 individual bones which are grouped into two general divisions; axial skeleton, the basic framework of the body and appendicular skeleton, the extremities.\n\nThe human skull is composed of 22 bones, besides 6 tiny ear bones and one hyoid bone.\n\nIn human, there are 33 vertebrae. Seven cervical vertebrae, 12 thoracic vertebrae; 5 lumber vertebrae; 5 sacral vertebrae and at the end of the vertebral column is the coccyx or tail bone which consists of 4 small fused vertebrae.\n\nIn human there are twelve pairs of ribs, one pair articulating with each of the thoracic vertebrae forming a cage that encloses the heart and lungs.\n\nThe appendicular skeleton consists of pectoral girdles with forelimbs and pelvic girdle with hind limbs.\n\nThe sites where two or more bones meet are called joints or articulations.\n\nDeformities of skeleton may be genetic, hormonal or due to the effects of nutrient deficiency.\n\nA fracture means broken bone.\n\nSimple fractures are those in which the skin is intact.\n\nCompound fracture is an open fracture: if the bone ends penetrate the skin and form a wound.\n\nA dislocated joint is a joint that slips out of place.\n\nBased on location, three types of muscles are skeletal, cardiac, and smooth.\n\nThe skeletal muscle consists of muscles bundle, which are further composed of huge elongated cells called muscles fibre.\n\nThe immediate source of energy for the muscle contraction is ATP, stored in the muscle cells. An enzyme ATPase, in the muscle cells breaks ATP to ADP, thus releasing energy for muscle contraction.\n\nMuscle fatigue is a condition of the muscle in which its capacity to produce maximum contraction is reduced even though the muscle still may be receiving stimuli.\n\nMuscle cramps are sudden, involuntary contractions or spasms in one or more muscles.\n\nTetany is a symptom characterized by muscle cramps, spasms or tremors.\n\nTetanus is a infection of the nervous system caused by a deadly bacteria Clostridium tetani.";
BIOLOGY_12_DATA[2]["urduSummary"]="حیوانوں کا ڈھانچا کششِ ثقل اور بیرونی قوتوں کے مقابل جسم کو سہارا دیتا ہے۔\n\nفقاریوں کا ڈھانچا غضروف یا ہڈی سے بنتا ہے۔\n\nغضروف کونڈروسائٹس اور ٹائپ II کولیجن کے مضبوط لچک دار میٹرکس سے بنتا ہے؛ اس میں خون کی نالیاں نہیں۔\n\nہڈی زندہ سخت اور مضبوط ساخت ہے، جو سخت بنیادی مادے یا میٹرکس اور خلیوں سے بنتی ہے۔\n\nہڈیوں میں ہڈی بنانے والے اوسٹیوبلاسٹس، پرانی ہڈی تحلیل کرنے والے اوسٹیوکلاسٹس اور پختہ اوسٹیوبلاسٹس یعنی اوسٹیوسائٹس ہوتے ہیں۔\n\nانسانی ڈھانچے کی 206 ہڈیاں دو حصوں میں ہیں: محوری ڈھانچا، جسم کا بنیادی فریم، اور ضمیمی ڈھانچا، اطراف کے اعضا۔\n\nانسانی کھوپڑی میں 22 ہڈیاں، اس کے علاوہ کان کی 6 چھوٹی ہڈیاں اور ایک ہائیوئڈ ہیں۔\n\nانسان میں 33 مہرے ہیں: 7 عنقی، 12 صدری، 5 قطنی، 5 سیکرل اور آخر میں 4 چھوٹے جڑے مہروں کا کوکسکس یا دم کی ہڈی۔\n\nانسان میں پسلیوں کے بارہ جوڑے ہیں، ہر صدری مہرے سے ایک جوڑا جڑ کر دل اور پھیپھڑوں کا پنجرہ بناتا ہے۔\n\nضمیمی ڈھانچا اگلے اعضا کے ساتھ کندھوں کی پٹیوں اور پچھلے اعضا کے ساتھ شرونی پٹی سے بنتا ہے۔\n\nدو یا زیادہ ہڈیوں کے ملنے کی جگہیں جوڑ ہیں۔\n\nڈھانچے کے بگاڑ موروثی، ہارمونی یا غذائی کمی سے ہو سکتے ہیں۔\n\nفریکچر کا مطلب ٹوٹی ہڈی ہے۔\n\nسادہ فریکچر میں جلد سلامت رہتی ہے۔\n\nمرکب فریکچر کھلا فریکچر ہے، جب ہڈی کے سرے جلد پھاڑ کر زخم بنائیں۔\n\nاترا جوڑ اپنی جگہ سے نکل جاتا ہے۔\n\nمقام کے لحاظ سے عضلات کی تین اقسام ڈھانچائی، قلبی اور ہموار ہیں۔\n\nڈھانچائی عضلہ گچھوں سے بنتا ہے، جو عضلاتی ریشے نامی بڑے لمبے خلیوں پر مشتمل ہیں۔\n\nعضلاتی سکڑاؤ کی فوری توانائی خلیوں میں محفوظ ATP ہے۔ ATPase اسے ADP میں توڑ کر توانائی خارج کرتا ہے۔\n\nعضلاتی تھکن میں تحریک ملنے کے باوجود زیادہ سے زیادہ سکڑاؤ کی صلاحیت کم ہوتی ہے۔\n\nعضلاتی اینٹھن ایک یا زیادہ عضلات کا اچانک غیر اختیاری سکڑاؤ یا تشنج ہے۔\n\nٹیٹنی کی علامت عضلاتی اینٹھن، تشنج یا لرزش ہے۔\n\nتشنج مہلک بیکٹیریا کلوسٹریڈیم ٹیٹانی سے اعصابی نظام کا انفیکشن ہے۔";
BIOLOGY_12_DATA[2]["pashtoSummary"]="د حیواناتو اسکلېټ د جاذبې او نورو بهرنیو ځواکونو پر وړاندې د بدن په ساتلو کې مرسته کوي.\n\nد فقاریه اسکلېټ له غضروف یا هډوکي جوړ دی.\n\nغضروف کونډروسایټونه او د دوهم ډول کولاژن کلک انعطاف منونکی ماتریکس لري او د وینې رګونه نه لري.\n\nهډوکی ژوندی کلک او پیاوړی جوړښت دی چې له کلکې بنسټیزې مادې یا ماتریکس او حجرو جوړ دی.\n\nهډوکي اوستیوبلاسټونه (هډوکی جوړوي)، اوستیوکلاسټونه (زوړ هډوکی ماتوي) او اوستیوسایټونه، چې پاخه اوستیوبلاسټونه دي، لري.\n\nد انسان د اسکلېټ 206 هډوکي په دوو برخو وېشل کېږي: محوري اسکلېټ، د بدن بنسټیز چوکاټ، او ضمیموي اسکلېټ، اندامونه.\n\nد انسان کوپړۍ 22 هډوکي لري، سربېره پر دې د غوږ 6 واړه هډوکي او یو هایویډ هډوکی شته.\n\nپه انسان کې 33 مهرې دي: 7 عنقي، 12 صدري، 5 قطني، 5 سکرل او په پای کې کوکسیکس یا د لکۍ هډوکی چې له 4 وړو یوځای شوو مهرو جوړ دی.\n\nپه انسان کې د پښتيو دولس جوړې دي، چې له هرې صدري مهرې سره یوه جوړه نښلي او د زړه او سږو شاوخوا قفس جوړوي.\n\nضمیموي اسکلېټ د مخکنیو اندامونو سره صدري کمربندونه او د شاتنیو اندامونو سره حوضي کمربند لري.\n\nد دوو یا زیاتو هډوکو د یوځای کېدو ځایونه بندونه دي.\n\nد اسکلېټ بدشکلۍ جنتیکي، هورموني یا د غذايي موادو د کمښت له امله کېدای شي.\n\nفریکچر د مات هډوکي معنا لري.\n\nپه ساده ماتېدنه کې پوستکی روغ وي.\n\nمرکبه ماتېدنه پرانیستې ماتېدنه ده، چې د هډوکي سرونه پوستکی سوری کړي او زخم جوړ کړي.\n\nبې ځایه شوی بند له خپل ځایه وځي.\n\nد موقعیت له مخې عضلې درې ډوله دي: اسکلېټي، قلبي او نرمې.\n\nاسکلېټي عضله له ګېډیو جوړه ده چې د عضلاتي ریشو په نوم لویې اوږدې حجرې لري.\n\nد عضلاتي انقباض د انرژۍ سمدستي سرچینه په حجرو کې ساتل شوی ATP دی. ATPase یې په ADP ماتوي او د انقباض انرژي خوشې کوي.\n\nپه عضلاتي ستړیا کې د تحریکونو له رسېدو سره سره د اعظمي انقباض وړتیا کمېږي.\n\nعضلاتي کرامپونه په یوه یا زیاتو عضلو کې ناڅاپي غیر ارادي انقباضونه یا تشنجونه دي.\n\nټیټني هغه نښه ده چې عضلاتي کرامپونه، تشنجونه یا رپېدل پکې وي.\n\nتیتانوس د وژونکې بکتریا کلوسټریډیم ټیټاني له امله د عصبي سیسټم انتان دی.";
BIOLOGY_12_DATA[2].unreviewedExercise=BIOLOGY_12_DATA[2].exercise;BIOLOGY_12_DATA[2].exercise={"mcqs": [{"qNo": "i", "q": "The disorder in which bones are porous and thin but bone composition is normal is;", "opts": ["osteomalacia", "osteoporosis", "rickets", "arthritis"], "ans": 1, "explanation": "Osteoporosis involves loss of bone mass and deterioration of its structure, making bones thinner and more fragile.", "sourceReferences": [{"printedPage": 81}], "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning answer"}, {"qNo": "ii", "q": "The organic portion of bone’s matrix is important in providing all but;", "opts": ["tensile strength", "hardness", "to resist stretch", "flexibility"], "ans": 1, "explanation": "Organic collagen contributes flexibility and tensile resistance; mineral salts provide the characteristic hardness.", "sourceReferences": [{"printedPage": 81}], "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning answer"}, {"qNo": "iii", "q": "The remodeling of bone is a function of which cells?", "opts": ["chondrocytes and osteocytes", "osteoblasts and osteoclasts", "chondroblasts and osteoclasts", "osteoblasts and osteocytes"], "ans": 1, "explanation": "Osteoclasts resorb old bone and osteoblasts form new bone during remodeling.", "sourceReferences": [{"printedPage": 81}], "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning answer"}, {"qNo": "iv", "q": "In skeletal muscle, calcium facilitates contraction by binding to", "opts": ["tropomyosin", "actin.", "troponin.", "myosin."], "ans": 2, "explanation": "Calcium binds troponin, allowing tropomyosin to shift and expose actin’s myosin-binding sites.", "sourceReferences": [{"printedPage": 81}], "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning answer"}, {"qNo": "v", "q": "Which of the following statements concerning the role of Ca²⁺ in the contraction of skeletal muscle is correct?", "opts": ["The mitochondria act as a store of Ca²⁺ for the contractile process", "Ca²⁺ entry across the plasma membrane is important in sustaining the contraction of skeletal muscle", "A rise in intracellular Ca²⁺ allows actin to interact with myosin", "The tension of a skeletal muscle fibre is partly regulated by G proteins"], "ans": 2, "explanation": "Increased calcium in the muscle-fibre cytoplasm permits the actin–myosin interaction through the regulatory proteins.", "sourceReferences": [{"printedPage": 81}], "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning answer"}, {"qNo": "vi", "q": "The function of the T tubules in muscle contraction is to", "opts": ["make and store glycogen", "release Ca²⁺ into the cell interior and then pick it up again", "make the action potential deep into the muscle cells", "to hamper the nerve impulse"], "ans": 2, "explanation": "T tubules conduct the action potential into the fibre; the sarcoplasmic reticulum stores, releases and takes up calcium.", "sourceReferences": [{"printedPage": 81}], "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning answer"}, {"qNo": "vii", "q": "The sites where the motor nerve impulse is transmitted from the nerve endings to the skeletal muscle cell membranes are the:", "opts": ["neuromuscular junctions", "sarcomeres", "myofilaments", "Z discs"], "ans": 0, "explanation": "A neuromuscular junction is the communication site between a motor neuron and a skeletal muscle fibre.", "sourceReferences": [{"printedPage": 81}], "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning answer"}, {"qNo": "viii", "q": "Myoglobin has a special function in muscle tissue.", "opts": ["it breaks down glycogen", "it is a contractile protein", "it holds a reserve supply of oxygen in the muscle", "none of these"], "ans": 2, "explanation": "Myoglobin binds oxygen and provides a reserve in muscle cells.", "sourceReferences": [{"printedPage": 82}], "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning answer"}], "shortQuestions": [{"qNo": "i", "q": "Name the cranial and facial bones.", "steps": ["Cranial bones: frontal, occipital, sphenoid and ethmoid are unpaired; parietal and temporal are paired. This gives eight cranial bones.", "Facial bones: mandible and vomer are unpaired; maxilla, zygomatic, lacrimal, nasal, inferior nasal concha and palatine are paired. This gives fourteen facial bones."], "answer": "<div><strong>SpaceBook learning answer</strong><ol><li data-example-step=\"0\" style=\"\">Cranial bones: frontal, occipital, sphenoid and ethmoid are unpaired; parietal and temporal are paired. This gives eight cranial bones.</li><li data-example-step=\"1\" style=\"display:none;\">Facial bones: mandible and vomer are unpaired; maxilla, zygomatic, lacrimal, nasal, inferior nasal concha and palatine are paired. This gives fourteen facial bones.</li></ol><button type=\"button\" data-revealed=\"1\" onclick=\"revealMathExampleStep(this)\">Next step</button> <button type=\"button\" onclick=\"resetMathExampleSteps(this)\">Start again</button></div><div data-example-answer style=\"display:none;margin-top:.7rem;\"><strong>Answer: </strong>Eight cranial bones form the cranium and fourteen facial bones form the face, as listed in Table16.3.</div>", "plainAnswer": "Eight cranial bones form the cranium and fourteen facial bones form the face, as listed in Table16.3.", "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning answer", "sourceReferences": [{"printedPage": 82}]}, {"qNo": "ii", "q": "What is the function of the intervertebral discs?", "steps": ["The discs lie between adjacent vertebral bodies.", "Their resilient structure absorbs shocks and distributes loads.", "They also allow limited movement while helping connect and support the vertebral column."], "answer": "<div><strong>SpaceBook learning answer</strong><ol><li data-example-step=\"0\" style=\"\">The discs lie between adjacent vertebral bodies.</li><li data-example-step=\"1\" style=\"display:none;\">Their resilient structure absorbs shocks and distributes loads.</li><li data-example-step=\"2\" style=\"display:none;\">They also allow limited movement while helping connect and support the vertebral column.</li></ol><button type=\"button\" data-revealed=\"1\" onclick=\"revealMathExampleStep(this)\">Next step</button> <button type=\"button\" onclick=\"resetMathExampleSteps(this)\">Start again</button></div><div data-example-answer style=\"display:none;margin-top:.7rem;\"><strong>Answer: </strong>Intervertebral discs cushion the vertebrae, absorb shocks and permit controlled flexibility.</div>", "plainAnswer": "Intervertebral discs cushion the vertebrae, absorb shocks and permit controlled flexibility.", "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning answer", "sourceReferences": [{"printedPage": 82}]}, {"qNo": "iv", "q": "Briefly describe the impairment of function seen in cleft palate.", "steps": ["A cleft palate leaves an opening in the roof of the mouth.", "This can make feeding difficult and interfere with normal speech.", "Associated middle-ear problems may affect hearing."], "answer": "<div><strong>SpaceBook learning answer</strong><ol><li data-example-step=\"0\" style=\"\">A cleft palate leaves an opening in the roof of the mouth.</li><li data-example-step=\"1\" style=\"display:none;\">This can make feeding difficult and interfere with normal speech.</li><li data-example-step=\"2\" style=\"display:none;\">Associated middle-ear problems may affect hearing.</li></ol><button type=\"button\" data-revealed=\"1\" onclick=\"revealMathExampleStep(this)\">Next step</button> <button type=\"button\" onclick=\"resetMathExampleSteps(this)\">Start again</button></div><div data-example-answer style=\"display:none;margin-top:.7rem;\"><strong>Answer: </strong>Cleft palate can impair feeding, speech and hearing; effects vary between individuals.</div><p>Source: <a href=\"https://www.gosh.nhs.uk/conditions-and-treatments/conditions-we-treat/cleft-lip-and-palate/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Great Ormond Street Hospital — cleft lip and palate</a>.</p>", "plainAnswer": "Cleft palate can impair feeding, speech and hearing; effects vary between individuals.", "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning answer", "sourceReferences": [{"printedPage": 82}]}], "longQuestions": [{"qNo": "i", "q": "Describe the structure of bone.", "steps": ["Bone is living connective tissue containing cells embedded in a hard, mineralized matrix.", "Its organic component is chiefly collagen, which contributes flexibility and tensile strength. Inorganic calcium-phosphate-rich mineral gives hardness.", "Osteoblasts produce bone matrix; osteocytes are mature bone cells within it; osteoclasts resorb bone during remodeling.", "Bone has blood vessels that support its living cells and metabolic exchange.", "The chapter gives two approximate organic/inorganic ratios in different paragraphs; use the qualitative distinction between collagen and mineral rather than treating the two printed ratios as identical."], "answer": "<div><strong>SpaceBook learning answer</strong><ol><li data-example-step=\"0\" style=\"\">Bone is living connective tissue containing cells embedded in a hard, mineralized matrix.</li><li data-example-step=\"1\" style=\"display:none;\">Its organic component is chiefly collagen, which contributes flexibility and tensile strength. Inorganic calcium-phosphate-rich mineral gives hardness.</li><li data-example-step=\"2\" style=\"display:none;\">Osteoblasts produce bone matrix; osteocytes are mature bone cells within it; osteoclasts resorb bone during remodeling.</li><li data-example-step=\"3\" style=\"display:none;\">Bone has blood vessels that support its living cells and metabolic exchange.</li><li data-example-step=\"4\" style=\"display:none;\">The chapter gives two approximate organic/inorganic ratios in different paragraphs; use the qualitative distinction between collagen and mineral rather than treating the two printed ratios as identical.</li></ol><button type=\"button\" data-revealed=\"1\" onclick=\"revealMathExampleStep(this)\">Next step</button> <button type=\"button\" onclick=\"resetMathExampleSteps(this)\">Start again</button></div><div data-example-answer style=\"display:none;margin-top:.7rem;\"><strong>Answer: </strong>Bone consists of living cells in a collagen-and-mineral matrix and is continually maintained and remodeled.</div><p>Reference: <a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/6-3-bone-structure\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OpenStax — bone structure</a>.</p>", "plainAnswer": "Bone consists of living cells in a collagen-and-mineral matrix and is continually maintained and remodeled.", "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning answer", "sourceReferences": [{"printedPage": 82}]}, {"qNo": "ii", "q": "Describe major divisions of human axial skeleton", "steps": ["The skull protects the brain and includes the cranial and facial bones, with associated ear ossicles and hyoid discussed in the chapter.", "The vertebral column supports the trunk and protects the spinal cord. Its regions are cervical, thoracic, lumbar, sacral and coccygeal.", "The thoracic cage consists of ribs and sternum and protects the heart and lungs.", "There are twelve rib pairs: seven attach directly to the sternum, three indirectly, and the final two have no anterior attachment."], "answer": "<div><strong>SpaceBook learning answer</strong><ol><li data-example-step=\"0\" style=\"\">The skull protects the brain and includes the cranial and facial bones, with associated ear ossicles and hyoid discussed in the chapter.</li><li data-example-step=\"1\" style=\"display:none;\">The vertebral column supports the trunk and protects the spinal cord. Its regions are cervical, thoracic, lumbar, sacral and coccygeal.</li><li data-example-step=\"2\" style=\"display:none;\">The thoracic cage consists of ribs and sternum and protects the heart and lungs.</li><li data-example-step=\"3\" style=\"display:none;\">There are twelve rib pairs: seven attach directly to the sternum, three indirectly, and the final two have no anterior attachment.</li></ol><button type=\"button\" data-revealed=\"1\" onclick=\"revealMathExampleStep(this)\">Next step</button> <button type=\"button\" onclick=\"resetMathExampleSteps(this)\">Start again</button></div><div data-example-answer style=\"display:none;margin-top:.7rem;\"><strong>Answer: </strong>The axial skeleton comprises the skull, vertebral column and thoracic cage.</div>", "plainAnswer": "The axial skeleton comprises the skull, vertebral column and thoracic cage.", "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning answer", "sourceReferences": [{"printedPage": 82}]}, {"qNo": "iii", "q": "What are types of fractures? Describe the repair process of a simple fracture.", "steps": ["A simple or closed fracture leaves the skin intact; an open or compound fracture communicates with a skin wound.", "Hematoma formation: torn vessels bleed and a clot forms at the break.", "Fibrocartilaginous callus formation: new vessels and repair cells enter; a soft callus bridges the broken ends.", "Bony callus formation: the soft callus is replaced by a harder bridge of spongy bone.", "Remodeling: bone is reorganized in response to mechanical stresses, restoring its structure."], "answer": "<div><strong>SpaceBook learning answer</strong><ol><li data-example-step=\"0\" style=\"\">A simple or closed fracture leaves the skin intact; an open or compound fracture communicates with a skin wound.</li><li data-example-step=\"1\" style=\"display:none;\">Hematoma formation: torn vessels bleed and a clot forms at the break.</li><li data-example-step=\"2\" style=\"display:none;\">Fibrocartilaginous callus formation: new vessels and repair cells enter; a soft callus bridges the broken ends.</li><li data-example-step=\"3\" style=\"display:none;\">Bony callus formation: the soft callus is replaced by a harder bridge of spongy bone.</li><li data-example-step=\"4\" style=\"display:none;\">Remodeling: bone is reorganized in response to mechanical stresses, restoring its structure.</li></ol><button type=\"button\" data-revealed=\"1\" onclick=\"revealMathExampleStep(this)\">Next step</button> <button type=\"button\" onclick=\"resetMathExampleSteps(this)\">Start again</button></div><div data-example-answer style=\"display:none;margin-top:.7rem;\"><strong>Answer: </strong>The two source fracture types are closed and open; repair proceeds through hematoma, soft callus, bony callus and remodeling.</div>", "plainAnswer": "The two source fracture types are closed and open; repair proceeds through hematoma, soft callus, bony callus and remodeling.", "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning answer", "sourceReferences": [{"printedPage": 82}]}, {"qNo": "iv", "q": "Compare smooth, cardiac, and skeletal muscles.", "steps": ["Skeletal muscle attaches to the skeleton, has long unbranched striated fibres with many nuclei, and is normally under voluntary control. It moves the body and maintains posture.", "Cardiac muscle occurs in the heart, is striated and branched, and contracts involuntarily and rhythmically to pump blood. The source describes a single nucleus.", "Smooth muscle occurs in walls of hollow organs and vessels. Its spindle-shaped cells have a central nucleus, lack striations and are involuntary."], "answer": "<div><strong>SpaceBook learning answer</strong><ol><li data-example-step=\"0\" style=\"\">Skeletal muscle attaches to the skeleton, has long unbranched striated fibres with many nuclei, and is normally under voluntary control. It moves the body and maintains posture.</li><li data-example-step=\"1\" style=\"display:none;\">Cardiac muscle occurs in the heart, is striated and branched, and contracts involuntarily and rhythmically to pump blood. The source describes a single nucleus.</li><li data-example-step=\"2\" style=\"display:none;\">Smooth muscle occurs in walls of hollow organs and vessels. Its spindle-shaped cells have a central nucleus, lack striations and are involuntary.</li></ol><button type=\"button\" data-revealed=\"1\" onclick=\"revealMathExampleStep(this)\">Next step</button> <button type=\"button\" onclick=\"resetMathExampleSteps(this)\">Start again</button></div><div data-example-answer style=\"display:none;margin-top:.7rem;\"><strong>Answer: </strong>Skeletal: voluntary body movement; cardiac: involuntary heart pumping; smooth: involuntary action in hollow organs and vessels.</div>", "plainAnswer": "Skeletal: voluntary body movement; cardiac: involuntary heart pumping; smooth: involuntary action in hollow organs and vessels.", "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning answer", "sourceReferences": [{"printedPage": 82}]}, {"qNo": "v", "q": "Explain the ultra-structure of human skeletal muscles.", "steps": ["A skeletal muscle contains bundles of long muscle fibres. Each fibre has a sarcolemma surrounding sarcoplasm with peripheral nuclei, mitochondria, glycogen and myoglobin.", "T tubules carry excitation inward, while the sarcoplasmic reticulum stores and releases calcium.", "The fibre contains myofibrils composed of repeating sarcomeres between successive Z lines.", "Thin actin filaments and thick myosin filaments form the banding pattern; thin filaments also contain troponin and tropomyosin.", "The A band corresponds to the thick-filament length, the I band contains thin filaments without thick overlap, and the H zone is the central thick-only region. The M line lies centrally."], "answer": "<div><strong>SpaceBook learning answer</strong><ol><li data-example-step=\"0\" style=\"\">A skeletal muscle contains bundles of long muscle fibres. Each fibre has a sarcolemma surrounding sarcoplasm with peripheral nuclei, mitochondria, glycogen and myoglobin.</li><li data-example-step=\"1\" style=\"display:none;\">T tubules carry excitation inward, while the sarcoplasmic reticulum stores and releases calcium.</li><li data-example-step=\"2\" style=\"display:none;\">The fibre contains myofibrils composed of repeating sarcomeres between successive Z lines.</li><li data-example-step=\"3\" style=\"display:none;\">Thin actin filaments and thick myosin filaments form the banding pattern; thin filaments also contain troponin and tropomyosin.</li><li data-example-step=\"4\" style=\"display:none;\">The A band corresponds to the thick-filament length, the I band contains thin filaments without thick overlap, and the H zone is the central thick-only region. The M line lies centrally.</li></ol><button type=\"button\" data-revealed=\"1\" onclick=\"revealMathExampleStep(this)\">Next step</button> <button type=\"button\" onclick=\"resetMathExampleSteps(this)\">Start again</button></div><div data-example-answer style=\"display:none;margin-top:.7rem;\"><strong>Answer: </strong>Skeletal muscle is organized as muscle → bundles → fibres → myofibrils → sarcomeres → thick and thin filaments.</div>", "plainAnswer": "Skeletal muscle is organized as muscle → bundles → fibres → myofibrils → sarcomeres → thick and thin filaments.", "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning answer", "sourceReferences": [{"printedPage": 82}]}, {"qNo": "vi", "q": "Explain the sliding filament theory of contraction using appropriately labeled diagrams of a relaxed and a contracted sarcomere.", "steps": ["Calcium exposes binding sites through the thin-filament regulatory proteins.", "Myosin cross-bridges bind actin and pull thin filaments toward the centre. ATP supports repeated attachment, movement and detachment.", "The Z lines move closer together; the sarcomere, I bands and H zone shorten. The A band and individual filament lengths remain essentially unchanged.", "When excitation stops and calcium returns to the sarcoplasmic reticulum, active cross-bridge cycling stops and the fibre can relax."], "answer": "<div><strong>SpaceBook learning answer</strong><ol><li data-example-step=\"0\" style=\"\">Calcium exposes binding sites through the thin-filament regulatory proteins.</li><li data-example-step=\"1\" style=\"display:none;\">Myosin cross-bridges bind actin and pull thin filaments toward the centre. ATP supports repeated attachment, movement and detachment.</li><li data-example-step=\"2\" style=\"display:none;\">The Z lines move closer together; the sarcomere, I bands and H zone shorten. The A band and individual filament lengths remain essentially unchanged.</li><li data-example-step=\"3\" style=\"display:none;\">When excitation stops and calcium returns to the sarcoplasmic reticulum, active cross-bridge cycling stops and the fibre can relax.</li></ol><button type=\"button\" data-revealed=\"1\" onclick=\"revealMathExampleStep(this)\">Next step</button> <button type=\"button\" onclick=\"resetMathExampleSteps(this)\">Start again</button></div><div data-example-answer style=\"display:none;margin-top:.7rem;\"><strong>Answer: </strong>Contraction results from greater overlap of actin and myosin, not shortening of the filaments themselves.</div><figure><svg viewBox=\"0 0 600 300\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Relaxed and contracted sarcomeres: Z lines approach while actin and myosin retain their lengths\" style=\"width:100%;max-width:720px;background:#fff;color:#142838\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><style>text{font:14px sans-serif;fill:#142838}</style><text x=\"20\" y=\"30\">Relaxed</text><path d=\"M70 45v65 M530 45v65\" stroke=\"#182e43\" stroke-width=\"4\"/><path d=\"M70 55h170 M530 55h-170\" stroke=\"#168eab\" stroke-width=\"5\"/><path d=\"M70 85h170 M530 85h-170\" stroke=\"#168eab\" stroke-width=\"5\"/><path d=\"M200 70h200\" stroke=\"#b83c60\" stroke-width=\"10\"/><text x=\"62\" y=\"131\">Z</text><text x=\"522\" y=\"131\">Z</text><text x=\"283\" y=\"100\">M line</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"155\">Contracted</text><path d=\"M120 170v65 M480 170v65\" stroke=\"#182e43\" stroke-width=\"4\"/><path d=\"M120 180h170 M480 180h-170\" stroke=\"#168eab\" stroke-width=\"5\"/><path d=\"M120 210h170 M480 210h-170\" stroke=\"#168eab\" stroke-width=\"5\"/><path d=\"M200 195h200\" stroke=\"#b83c60\" stroke-width=\"10\"/><text x=\"112\" y=\"256\">Z</text><text x=\"472\" y=\"256\">Z</text><text x=\"283\" y=\"225\">M line</text><text x=\"35\" y=\"290\">Blue: thin actin</text><text x=\"220\" y=\"290\">Red: thick myosin</text><text x=\"430\" y=\"290\">Z–Z = sarcomere</text></svg><figcaption>SpaceBook schematic: overlap increases during contraction; filament lengths stay constant.</figcaption></figure><p>Mechanism reference: <a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/10-3-muscle-fiber-contraction-and-relaxation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OpenStax — muscle contraction and relaxation</a>.</p>", "plainAnswer": "Contraction results from greater overlap of actin and myosin, not shortening of the filaments themselves.", "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning answer", "sourceReferences": [{"printedPage": 82}]}, {"qNo": "vii", "q": "Describe the structure of a sarcomere and indicate the relationship of the sarcomere to the myofilament", "steps": ["A sarcomere extends from one Z line to the next and is a repeating contractile unit in a myofibril.", "Thin actin filaments attach to the Z lines and extend toward the centre; thick myosin filaments occupy the central region.", "Their arrangement creates I bands, an A band, an H zone and an M line.", "Myofilaments are the protein filaments within each sarcomere; many sarcomeres arranged end to end form a myofibril."], "answer": "<div><strong>SpaceBook learning answer</strong><ol><li data-example-step=\"0\" style=\"\">A sarcomere extends from one Z line to the next and is a repeating contractile unit in a myofibril.</li><li data-example-step=\"1\" style=\"display:none;\">Thin actin filaments attach to the Z lines and extend toward the centre; thick myosin filaments occupy the central region.</li><li data-example-step=\"2\" style=\"display:none;\">Their arrangement creates I bands, an A band, an H zone and an M line.</li><li data-example-step=\"3\" style=\"display:none;\">Myofilaments are the protein filaments within each sarcomere; many sarcomeres arranged end to end form a myofibril.</li></ol><button type=\"button\" data-revealed=\"1\" onclick=\"revealMathExampleStep(this)\">Next step</button> <button type=\"button\" onclick=\"resetMathExampleSteps(this)\">Start again</button></div><div data-example-answer style=\"display:none;margin-top:.7rem;\"><strong>Answer: </strong>A sarcomere is a structural unit built from overlapping thick and thin myofilaments, bounded by two Z lines.</div>", "plainAnswer": "A sarcomere is a structural unit built from overlapping thick and thin myofilaments, bounded by two Z lines.", "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning answer", "sourceReferences": [{"printedPage": 82}]}], "activities": [{"title": "Identify the bones of the pelvic girdle, pectoral girdle, arms and leg by using the model of human skeleton.", "desc": "<p><strong>SpaceBook learning answer:</strong> On the model, identify the paired hip bones (ilium, ischium and pubis), scapulae and clavicles, humerus, radius, ulna, femur, tibia and fibula. Then locate wrist, palm and finger bones and ankle, foot and toe bones. Use the model’s labels to distinguish the girdles from the free limbs.</p>", "sourceReferences": [{"printedPage": 82}], "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning answer"}, {"title": "Compare the structure of skeleton, smooth and cardiac muscles with the help of prepared slides.", "desc": "<p><strong>SpaceBook learning answer:</strong> Record the actual slides: skeletal muscle has long parallel striated fibres and multiple peripheral nuclei; cardiac muscle has branching striated cells and intercalated discs; smooth muscle has spindle-shaped cells, a central nucleus and no striations. Do not invent features that are not visible at the available magnification.</p>", "sourceReferences": [{"printedPage": 82}], "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning answer"}, {"title": "Draw a diagram of sarcomere and label its parts.", "desc": "<p><strong>SpaceBook learning answer:</strong> Draw two Z lines, thin actin filaments attached to each, and central thick myosin filaments. Label the sarcomere, A band, I bands, H zone, M line and the overlap region. The relaxed/contracted schematic in long answer vi provides a starting layout.</p>", "sourceReferences": [{"printedPage": 82}], "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning answer"}, {"title": "Justify how the main functions of the skeleton are to act as a system of rods and levers, which are moved by the muscles.", "desc": "<p><strong>SpaceBook learning answer:</strong> Bones act as rigid levers, joints provide pivot points and contracting muscles apply pulling forces through their attachments. The resulting lever movement changes the position of body parts. The skeleton also supports and protects the body.</p>", "sourceReferences": [{"printedPage": 82}], "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning answer"}, {"title": "Justify why do the muscles pull but do not push.", "desc": "<p><strong>SpaceBook learning answer:</strong> Muscle contraction develops tension that pulls on attachments. Active shortening does not push those attachments apart. Opposing muscles or external forces produce the reverse movement.</p>", "sourceReferences": [{"printedPage": 82}], "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning answer"}, {"title": "Relate the bipedal posture of man with his skeleton and musculature.", "desc": "<p><strong>SpaceBook learning answer:</strong> The vertebral column and pelvis support an upright trunk, the lower limbs bear weight, and the feet provide a base of support. Coordinated trunk, hip and leg muscles maintain balance and produce walking movements.</p>", "sourceReferences": [{"printedPage": 83}], "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning answer"}, {"title": "Name the techniques for joint transplantation.", "desc": "<p><strong>SpaceBook learning answer:</strong> The intended term is joint replacement or arthroplasty: partial replacement replaces part of a damaged joint, while total replacement replaces the relevant joint surfaces with artificial components. These are prosthetic replacements, rather than routine transplantation of an entire donor joint.</p><p>Source: <a href=\"https://www.niams.nih.gov/health-topics/osteoarthritis/diagnosis-treatment-and-steps-to-take\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIAMS — osteoarthritis surgery</a>.</p>", "sourceReferences": [{"printedPage": 83}], "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning answer"}, {"title": "Justify the use of calcium in teenage and twenties can be a preventive action against osteoporosis.", "desc": "<p><strong>SpaceBook learning answer:</strong> Adequate calcium and vitamin D support development of peak bone mass during growth and early adulthood. Building stronger bones provides a better reserve against later bone loss. Nutrition is one factor alongside physical activity and other influences; the question does not imply that everyone needs supplements.</p><p>Source: <a href=\"https://www.niams.nih.gov/health-topics/osteoporosis/diagnosis-treatment-and-steps-to-take\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NIAMS — osteoporosis and bone health</a>.</p>", "sourceReferences": [{"printedPage": 83}], "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning answer"}, {"title": "Reason out the rigor mortis.", "desc": "<p><strong>SpaceBook learning answer:</strong> After death, ATP production stops. Without fresh ATP, myosin cannot detach normally from actin, so cross-bridges remain attached and the muscles become stiff.</p><p>Source: <a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/10-3-muscle-fiber-contraction-and-relaxation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OpenStax — muscle contraction and relaxation</a>.</p>", "sourceReferences": [{"printedPage": 83}], "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning answer"}, {"title": "Relate improper posture to bone/joint problems.", "desc": "<p><strong>SpaceBook learning answer:</strong> Posture changes how body weight and muscle forces are distributed. Sustained awkward positioning can increase strain on particular muscles, ligaments and joints. Balanced alignment and changing position help distribute loads; posture alone does not explain every skeletal disorder.</p>", "sourceReferences": [{"printedPage": 83}], "solutionOrigin": "SpaceBook learning answer"}], "sourceStatus": "printed questions transcribed; learning answers supplied; full exercise translations pending", "sourceReferences": [{"printedPage": 81}, {"printedPage": 82}, {"printedPage": 83}], "sourceNotes": ["Short-question numbering in the scan skips iii; visible items i,ii,iv retained.", "An isolated word muscle follows MCQv options in the print; treated as a stray fragment, not an extra option."], "learningReferences": [{"title": "NIAMS — Osteoporosis", "url": "https://www.niams.nih.gov/health-topics/osteoporosis"}, {"title": "OpenStax — Bone Structure", "url": "https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/6-3-bone-structure"}, {"title": "OpenStax — Muscle Contraction and Relaxation", "url": "https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/10-3-muscle-fiber-contraction-and-relaxation"}]};BIOLOGY_12_DATA[2].unreviewedSloBank=BIOLOGY_12_DATA[2].sloBank;BIOLOGY_12_DATA[2].sloBank=BIOLOGY_12_DATA[2].exercise;
