// Unit 18 exercise from printed pp. 132–133 / JPG133–134.
BIOLOGY_12_DATA[4].unreviewedExercise=BIOLOGY_12_DATA[4].exercise;
BIOLOGY_12_DATA[4].exercise={
mcqs:[
{qNo:"i",q:"Which condition results from excess growth hormone (GH) in adults?",options:["Cushing’s disease","Acromegaly","Hyperthyroidism","Diabetes mellitus"],ans:1,explanation:"Excess GH after long-bone growth plates close causes acromegaly; excess during childhood can cause gigantism.",sourceReferences:[{printedPage:132}]},
{qNo:"ii",q:"Which hormone regulates the kidney’s retention of water?",options:["Prolactin","Oxytocin","Thyroxine","Vasopressin (ADH)"],ans:3,explanation:"ADH (vasopressin) increases water reabsorption in kidney tubules.",sourceReferences:[{printedPage:132}]},
{qNo:"iii",q:"Which hormone is not released by the anterior pituitary?",options:["Melanocyte-stimulating hormone","Gonadotropin-releasing hormone","Thyroid-stimulating hormone","Growth hormone"],ans:0,explanation:"MSH is associated with the intermediate pituitary. GnRH is released by the hypothalamus, so the printed item has two non-anterior-pituitary choices; option (a) is the textbook’s likely intended answer, but this MCQ is ambiguous.",sourceReferences:[{printedPage:132}],sourceNote:"Ambiguous: GnRH is hypothalamic as well; retain this caveat."},
{qNo:"iv",q:"Parathyroid hormone acts to ensure that:",options:["Calcium levels in blood never drop too low","Sodium levels in urine are constant","Potassium levels in blood do not escalate","Water concentration in blood is sufficient"],ans:0,explanation:"PTH is a major regulator that raises blood calcium when it falls.",sourceReferences:[{printedPage:132}]},
{qNo:"v",q:"The adrenal cortex produces:",options:["Adrenaline","Calcitonin","Epinephrine","Aldosterone"],ans:3,explanation:"Aldosterone is a mineralocorticoid produced by the adrenal cortex. Adrenaline/epinephrine is from the medulla.",sourceReferences:[{printedPage:132}]},
{qNo:"vi",q:"Oxytocin is secreted by the endocrine gland named:",options:["Pituitary gland","Thyroid gland","Parathyroid gland","Adrenal gland"],ans:0,explanation:"The posterior pituitary releases oxytocin into blood; it is synthesized in the hypothalamus.",sourceReferences:[{printedPage:132}]},
{qNo:"vii",q:"Deficiency of vasopressin (ADH) leads to a disorder in which kidneys have a reduced ability to absorb water:",options:["Diabetes mellitus","Diabetes insipidus","Goiter","Exophthalmic goiter"],ans:1,explanation:"Insufficient ADH action can cause diabetes insipidus, with impaired renal water conservation.",sourceReferences:[{printedPage:132}]},
{qNo:"viii",q:"The functions of oxytocin are to:",options:["Cause the uterus to contract","Induce labour","Stimulate milk ejection during nursing","All of the above"],ans:3,explanation:"Oxytocin participates in uterine contractions during labour and the milk-ejection reflex during nursing.",sourceReferences:[{printedPage:133}]},
{qNo:"ix",q:"In humans, MSH (melanocyte-stimulating hormone):",options:["Regulates primary skin colour","Causes the thyroid to produce thyroxin","Governs the rate of tanning","Is present at very low concentration"],ans:3,explanation:"The textbook’s expected choice is that circulating MSH concentration is very low in humans; MSH is involved in melanocyte activity, but the wording is simplified.",sourceReferences:[{printedPage:133}]}
],
shortQuestions:[
{qNo:"i",q:"Differentiate between endocrine and exocrine glands.",ans:"Endocrine glands have no ducts and release hormones into extracellular fluid and then blood/lymph for delivery to targets. Exocrine glands release products onto a surface or into a lumen, directly or through ducts."},
{qNo:"ii",q:"Why is the anterior pituitary called the master gland?",ans:"It secretes several tropic hormones, including TSH, ACTH, FSH and LH, that regulate other endocrine glands. The label is traditional; pituitary activity itself is controlled by hypothalamic signals and feedback."},
{qNo:"iii",q:"List the effects of hyperthyroidism.",ans:"The textbook lists increased metabolic activity, rapid or irregular heartbeat, nervousness, sweating, heat intolerance and weight loss; Graves’ disease may involve eye protrusion. These signs vary and require clinical assessment."},
{qNo:"iv",q:"Why is the pancreas known as a double gland?",ans:"It has exocrine tissue that secretes digestive juice through ducts into the intestine and endocrine islets that release hormones such as insulin and glucagon into blood."},
{qNo:"v",q:"Why is insulin vital for normal survival?",ans:"Insulin helps body cells take up and use glucose and promotes storage of excess glucose, keeping blood glucose within a workable range. Severe insulin deficiency or lack of insulin action disrupts metabolism and requires medical care."}
],
detailedQuestions:[
{qNo:"i",q:"Describe the chemical nature of hormones, giving examples of important hormones.",ans:"<p>Hormones are commonly grouped as (1) steroid hormones derived from cholesterol, such as cortisol, aldosterone, estrogen, progesterone and testosterone; (2) amino-acid derivatives, including thyroid hormones and catecholamines such as epinephrine; and (3) proteins or peptides, including insulin, glucagon, growth hormone, prolactin, ADH and oxytocin. Chemical class affects how a hormone is transported and how it binds its target receptor.</p>"},
{qNo:"ii",q:"Explain the roles of hormones secreted by the anterior lobe of the pituitary.",ans:"<ul><li>GH supports growth and protein synthesis.</li><li>TSH stimulates thyroid hormone secretion.</li><li>ACTH stimulates adrenal-cortex hormone secretion.</li><li>FSH supports ovarian follicle development/estrogen secretion and sperm production.</li><li>LH supports ovulation and corpus-luteum function and stimulates testosterone production.</li><li>Prolactin supports milk production.</li></ul>Hypothalamic signals and feedback regulate their release; the intermediate and posterior lobes are distinct."},
{qNo:"iii",q:"Discuss regulation of calcium level in the body and effects of hypercalcemia and hypocalcemia.",ans:"<p>When blood calcium is low, parathyroid glands release PTH, which raises calcium through actions on bone and kidneys and indirectly supports intestinal absorption. When calcium is high, thyroid C cells release calcitonin, which can reduce osteoclast activity and favour calcium deposition in bone; vitamin D and kidney function also contribute. Markedly abnormal calcium may affect nerves, muscles, bones, kidneys and heart rhythm. Symptoms alone do not identify the cause; laboratory assessment is needed.</p>"},
{qNo:"iv",q:"Describe hormones involved in regulation of reproductive functions.",ans:"<p>Hypothalamic GnRH regulates pituitary FSH and LH. FSH supports ovarian follicle development and estrogen secretion and supports sperm production; LH triggers ovulation and corpus-luteum hormone secretion and stimulates testosterone production in testes. Estrogens and progesterone regulate reproductive tissues and menstrual-cycle changes; testosterone supports male reproductive function and secondary sex characteristics. Prolactin supports milk production, while oxytocin supports uterine contraction and milk ejection.</p>"},
{qNo:"v",q:"Analyze feedback mechanism with an example from chemical coordination.",ans:"<p><strong>Negative feedback:</strong> high blood glucose stimulates pancreatic insulin release; uptake and storage lower glucose, reducing the original stimulus and insulin release. Low glucose promotes glucagon release, which helps raise it. This tends to restore a range. <strong>Positive feedback:</strong> cervical pressure promotes oxytocin release, which strengthens contractions and increases pressure until birth ends the stimulus.</p>"}
],
activities:[
{title:"Artificially synthesized steroids in sports",desc:"Anabolic-androgenic steroids can increase muscle mass but nonmedical use carries risks to cardiovascular, liver, reproductive, endocrine and mental health, and can disrupt natural hormone production. Discuss evidence, fair competition and safer training; use only medicines prescribed and monitored by a qualified clinician.",sourceReferences:[{printedPage:133}]},
{title:"Claims that short stature can be increased",desc:"Sample critical response: height depends on genetics, growth plates, nutrition and health. Growth hormone is appropriate only for specific diagnosed conditions under specialist care; after growth plates close it does not lengthen bones. Ask what evidence supports a company’s claim, whether the product is regulated, and whether risks and limitations are disclosed. Avoid unverified products.",sourceReferences:[{printedPage:133}]}
],sourceStatus:"Printed pp.132–133 transcribed; worked answer set supplied; source review and translations pending",sourceReferences:[{printedPage:132},{printedPage:133}],solutionOrigin:"SpaceBook worked learning answers"};
BIOLOGY_12_DATA[4].keyPoints=["The endocrine system coordinates many body cells through hormones.","Endocrine glands release hormones internally; exocrine glands release products onto surfaces or into ducts.","Hormones include steroids, amino-acid derivatives, and proteins or peptides.","Pituitary hormones regulate other endocrine glands and body functions.","The thyroid secretes T₃, T₄ and calcitonin.","Parathyroid hormone is a major regulator of blood calcium.","Pancreatic insulin and glucagon help regulate blood glucose.","Adrenal glands have a cortex and medulla that release different hormones.","Ovaries and testes produce reproductive hormones and gametes.","Feedback mechanisms regulate a process through its outputs; negative feedback stabilizes, positive feedback amplifies change."];
BIOLOGY_12_DATA[4].exercise.mcqs.forEach(q=>{q.sourceReferences=q.sourceReferences||[{printedPage:132}];});
// Start Unit 19 from printed p. 135 / scan JPG136.
{const add=(heading,title,headingUrdu,headingPashto,paras,page)=>{const x={heading,title,headingUrdu,urduTitle:headingUrdu,headingPashto,paras,text:paras.map(p=>p.text).join("\n\n"),urdu:paras.map(p=>p.urdu).join("\n\n"),pashto:paras.map(p=>p.pashto).join("\n\n"),sourceReferences:[{printedPage:page,jpg:`Biology Book for 12 class KPTBB_page_${page+1}.jpg`,status:"source transcribed"}],translationStatus:"aligned Urdu/Pashto supplied; manual review pending"};const a=BIOLOGY_12_DATA[5].sections;const i=a.findIndex(s=>s.heading===heading);if(i>=0)Object.assign(a[i],x);else a.push(x);};
add("19.0 Introduction","19.0 Introduction","تعارف","پېژندنه",[{text:"Behaviour is an organism’s response to one or more stimuli, which may originate inside or outside it. Behaviour includes what an animal does and how it does it; the scientific study of animal behaviour is ethology.",urdu:"رویّہ کسی محرک یا محرکات کے جواب میں جاندار کا ردعمل ہے؛ محرک اندرونی یا بیرونی ہو سکتا ہے۔ رویّے میں یہ شامل ہے کہ جانور کیا کرتا اور کیسے کرتا ہے۔ جانوروں کے رویّے کا سائنسی مطالعہ ایتھولوجی کہلاتا ہے۔",pashto:"چلند د یوه یا څو محرکونو پر وړاندې د ژوندي موجود غبرګون دی؛ محرک له دننه یا بهر څخه راتلای شي. چلند دا رانغاړي چې حیوان څه کوي او څنګه یې کوي. د حیواني چلند علم ایتولوژي بلل کېږي."}],135);
add("19.1 NATURE OF BEHAVIOR","19.1 NATURE OF BEHAVIOR","رویّے کی نوعیت","د چلند ماهیت",[{text:"Responses range from simple plant growth toward light to complex courtship, territorial defence and care of young. A stimulus must be sensed, processed and interpreted to produce a response.",urdu:"ردعمل روشنی کی طرف پودے کی سادہ بڑھوتری سے لے کر پیچیدہ ملاپ، علاقے کے دفاع اور بچوں کی دیکھ بھال تک پھیلے ہیں۔ ردعمل کے لیے محرک کا محسوس، پراسیس اور تشریح ہونا ضروری ہے۔",pashto:"غبرګونونه د رڼا پر لور د بوټي له ساده ودې څخه تر پېچلي جفت‌غوښتنې، د سیمې دفاع او د بچیانو ساتنې پورې رسېږي. د غبرګون لپاره محرک باید احساس، پروسس او تفسیر شي."}],135);
add("19.1.1 Behavior and stimuli:","19.1.1 Behavior and stimuli:","رویّہ اور محرکات","چلند او محرکونه",[{text:"The ability to respond to a stimulus varies from simple responses to complex patterns. What an organism does after stimulation is part of its behaviour; sensing and processing the stimulus precede the response.",urdu:"محرک کا جواب دینے کی صلاحیت سادہ ردعمل سے پیچیدہ نمونوں تک مختلف ہوتی ہے۔ تحریک کے بعد جاندار جو کرتا ہے وہ اس کے رویّے کا حصہ ہے؛ ردعمل سے پہلے محرک کو محسوس اور پراسیس کیا جاتا ہے۔",pashto:"محرک ته د غبرګون وړتیا له ساده غبرګون څخه تر پېچلو بڼو پورې توپیر لري. له تحریک وروسته د ژوندي موجود کړنه یې د چلند برخه ده؛ له غبرګون مخکې محرک احساس او پروسس کېږي."}],135);
add("19.1.2 Effect of genetics on behavior:","19.1.2 Effect of genetics on behavior:","رویّے پر جینیات کے اثرات","پر چلند د جنتیک اغېز",[{text:"Genetic variation can contribute to differences in behaviour within and among species. Behaviour may have survival value in some environments, while its expression also depends on experience and context; avoid treating complex human traits as determined by genes alone.",urdu:"جینیاتی فرق انواع کے اندر اور درمیان رویّے کے فرق میں حصہ ڈال سکتا ہے۔ بعض ماحول میں رویّہ بقا کے لیے مفید ہو سکتا ہے، مگر اس کا اظہار تجربے اور حالات پر بھی منحصر ہے؛ پیچیدہ انسانی صفات کو صرف جینز سے طے شدہ نہ سمجھیں۔",pashto:"جنتیکي توپیر د نوعو دننه او د نوعو ترمنځ د چلند په توپیر کې ونډه لرلای شي. په ځینو چاپېریالونو کې چلند د بقا ارزښت لري، خو څرګندونه یې پر تجربې او حالت هم اړه لري؛ پېچلې انساني ځانګړنې یوازې جینونو ته مه منسوبوئ."}],135);
add("19.1.3 Biological rhythms:","19.1.3 Biological rhythms:","حیاتیاتی اوقات","بیولوژیکي تالونه",[{text:"Biological rhythms are recurring cycles in living systems. They may repeat yearly, monthly, daily or with tides, and measurable animal characteristics can fluctuate in regular patterns.",urdu:"حیاتیاتی تال جاندار نظاموں میں بار بار دہرائے جانے والے چکر ہیں۔ یہ سالانہ، ماہانہ، روزانہ یا مدوجزر کے ساتھ دہرائے جا سکتے ہیں اور جانوروں کی قابلِ پیمائش خصوصیات باقاعدہ نمونوں میں بدل سکتی ہیں۔",pashto:"بیولوژیکي تالونه په ژوندي سیستمونو کې تکراري دورې دي. کلنۍ، میاشتنی، ورځني یا د مدوجزر له مخې تکرارېدای شي، او د حیواناتو د اندازه کېدو وړ ځانګړنې په منظم ډول بدلېدای شي."}],135);
}
